<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  TEAM (development-signed) entitlements: scripts/install-dev.sh --team <ID>. This is the path
  that should load with SIP ENABLED. Apple DTS: DriverKit has "development" entitlement variants
  that are "available on all paid developer accounts without any special approval" and "allow a
  DEXT to match against any hardware". Xcode 16+ automatic signing covers the PCI family for
  development (distribution signing of PCI/USB still needs Apple's grant; that is spec M6).
  (developer.apple.com/forums/thread/809202)

  The PCI value is Apple's development WILDCARD (0xFFFFFFFF&0x00000000), exactly as DTS gives it:
  the development profile grants that value, and a hand-narrowed value risks a profile/entitlement
  mismatch. We are still scoped to Tenstorrent hardware twice over: Info.plist's personality matches
  only 0xb1401e52, and TTBlackholeDriver::Start re-checks vendor/device before touching anything.
  Clients get in through allow-any-userclient-access, the "DriverKit Allow Any UserClient
  (development)" capability on this identifier. 2026-10-21: on a new individual team the host app's
  com.apple.developer.driverkit.userclient-access "requires approval from Apple", while the
  development allow-any variant is self-service in the developer portal. Any local process may
  open the service, but only one at a time (NewUserClient refuses a second), and the broker is
  the one that normally holds it. Shipping (spec M6) switches back to userclient-access on the app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit.transport.pci</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>IOPCIPrimaryMatch</key>
			<string>0xFFFFFFFF&amp;0x00000000</string>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit.allow-any-userclient-access</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
